  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $70B in contracts in FY2026 to date, across 59,844 awards with an average award size of $1.2M. Obligations fell 10.6% year over year. Direct health and medical insurance carriers accounted for the single largest share of FY2026 to date obligations at $29.4B, and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing followed at $11.1B. Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) received $7.6B, while Computer Systems Design Services and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction received $3.4B and $2.5B respectively. The largest single vendors were OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. at $19.9B and MCKESSON CORPORATION at $10.8B. TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP received $9.8B, and the rest of the top-ten vendors range from $2.2B to $548M. Fiscal-year comparisons show obligations rose from $47.7B in FY21 to $78.3B in FY25 before the FY2026 to date total of $70B. All figures are contract obligations; current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How is VA spending split across major procurement areas?",
        "body": "Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest NAICS category at $29.4B. Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing and Offices of Physicians (except Mental Health Specialists) follow at $11.1B and $7.6B. Computer Systems Design Services, Commercial and Institutional Building Construction, and Other Computer Related Services account for $3.4B, $2.5B, and $2.1B respectively."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who captures the most VA contract dollars in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "OPTUM PUBLIC SECTOR SOLUTIONS, INC. leads vendor obligations at $19.9B, followed by MCKESSON CORPORATION at $10.8B and TRIWEST HEALTHCARE ALLIANCE CORP at $9.8B. Additional top vendors include QTC MEDICAL SERVICES INC at $2.2B, OPTUMSERVE HEALTH SERVICES, INC. at $1.8B, and LOYAL SOURCE GOVERNMENT SERVICES LLC at $1.8B."
      }
    ]
